<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamid Idris Awate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hamid Idris Awate was an Eritrean fighter regarded as the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>'s armed struggle for independence. On 1 September 1961, at Mount Adal in western Eritrea, he and a small band fired the first shots of the war against Ethiopian rule under the banner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a>. He died in 1962, and the anniversary of the first shots is a national holiday in Eritrea.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Awate was born in Gerset, between Tessenei and Golluj in south-western Italian Eritrea, and was of Beni-Amer descent. Accounts of his early life differ. According to many sources he served as an Eritrean ascari in the Italian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and the East African Campaign before deserting after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a>; others say he was a non-commissioned officer in the Sudan Defence Force during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, or a farmer in the Ghinda area before becoming a shifta, an outlaw, in 1941.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the early 1940s he settled in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, gathered a band of 50 men and raided the Kunama and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> from 1942 to 1948. The British authorities offered a large reward for his capture, and in 1951 he and his men surrendered after the British agreed not to prosecute him. After 1956 he took up arms again, in response to attacks by Christian highland shifta and in protest at the land policy of the Ethiopian officials who had taken control of Eritrea.</p><p>In July 1960 Eritrean students and intellectuals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a>, and Awate was persuaded, possibly by his fellow Beni-Amer Idris Mohammed Adam, to continue his fight under its banner; he asked for money and weapons and received a few old rifles. Warned by a Muslim in the police of a plan to arrest him in August 1961, he fled to Mount Adal, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agordat">Agordat</a>. On 1 September 1961 eleven fighters under his command attacked police posts in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, and a fight of 30 minutes ended in stalemate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 27 May 1962 Awate told his unit that he felt unwell, and he died the next morning; by one account he first gave his gun to Kiboob Hajaj, urging the continuation of the revolution. His companions buried him in secret and kept his death hidden for four years. The Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> raised a statue at his grave on 1 September 1994, and on 16 October 2011 the Italian town of Cologno Monzese dedicated a tree to him in its Garden of the Just of the World.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hamid Idris Awate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nationalists organised the Eritrean Liberation Movement in 1958 and the Eritrean Liberation Front in 1960, whose fighters under <b>Hamid Idris Awate</b> began armed struggle in September 1961.</blockquote><a href="/state/eritrea-federation">Eritrea (federation)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Italian Eritrea. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=15.48,38.25&amp;year=1943" aria-label="Eritrea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Hamid Idris Awate"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5678 6204h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5678 6204h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=15.48,38.25&amp;year=1943">What was here in 1943? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
